red either free of charge or at a lower price by the federal government in this way if individuals choose to purchase these types of services they will be doing so as informed consumers the deceptive mailings prevention act of 1989 is not designed to limit access to services by individuals or to prohibit solicitations by nongovernmental organizations nor is it designed to penalize legitimate organizations indeed organizations that have been chosen as agents or licensees of the federal government are specifically exempt from the provisions of this bill the goal of the deceptive mailings prevention act of 1989 is 4o assure that those who receive mailings are not misled into believing that free services can only be obtained for a fee or that services products or sollcitations have been endorsed by the federal government we owe it to all of our citizens to provide protections against the misuse of the mail i applaud my colleagues for their strong statement on this issue last year and i look forward to your equally strong support again in this congress the text of h r - follows r r be it enacted by the senate and rouse of representatives of the united states of america in congress assembled s10cron 1 siorttitl this -act may be cited as the deceptive mailings prevention act of 1989 e 11,1980s
1010000370,1989,,Mr. Speaker. Mayor George Livingston of Richmond. CA. has been a close friend of mine for many years as he has served with great distinction. as councilman and mayor of the city In which I was born. Over the course of the past 3 years. during which he has served as mayor. he has lee. the effort to revitalize the city. and with Impressive results. Economic development. transportation Improvements. and administrative Innovations are earning Richmond long overdue recognition. and improving the quality of government and of life for Richmonds residents. A great deal of the credit for those accomplishments are attributable to the work of Major Livingston. and the leadership he has provided together with members of the city council. and other elected leaders and private citizens In the city. Mayor Livingstons outstanding record was Justifiable praised In a recent editorial In the West County Times. which I would like to share with Members of the House today. The editorial. from the December 15. 1988. edition of the West County Times. follows:,mayor george livingston of richmond ca has been a close friend of mine for many years as he has served with great distinction as councilman and mayor of the city in which i was born over the course of the past 3 years during which he has served as mayor he has lee the effort to revitalize the city and with impressive results economic development transportation improvements and administrative innovations are earning richmond long overdue recognition and improving the quality of government and of life for richmonds residents a great deal of the credit for those accomplishments are attributable to the work of major livingston and the leadership he has provided together with members of the city council and other elected leaders and private citizens in the city mayor livingstons outstanding record was justifiable praised in a recent editorial in the west county times which i would like to share with members of the house today the editorial from the december 15 1988 edition of the west county times follows,1980s
1010000371,1989,,Mr. Speaker. the 101st Congress convenes today faced with a number of pressing national problems. None. however. carries more potential for damage to our economy than the growing crisis in the thrift Industry. The events of the past 10 days. In which the Federal Home Loan Bank Board sold dozens of savings and loans at firesale prices. only underscores the extent of the problem. During 1988. the Board took over 222 thrifts. with assets totaling $90 billion. The cost to the Federal Governmentthat Is the American taxpayeramounts to a minimum of $38.6 billion. and probably a good deal more In future years.. During the previous Congress. my House Banking Committee delved Into the S&L crisis and began the difficult work to find a solution. Legislation was approved to give the Board authority for stopgap measures to prevent a collapse of the Federal Savings and Loan Insurance Corporation. which liabilities for insured accounts in troubled institutions now amount to $60 to $80 billion more than Its assets. But it Is the spectacle of last weeks financial feeding frenzy at the Bank Board which brings home the nature of the problem. and the need for new leadership In this Issue. Contrary to the blandly reassuring statements by the Bank Board. the sales of bankE 12 January 3. 1989 CONGRE rupt S&Ls do not end the Federal Governments liability. First. in many of the deals the Bank Board retains part ownership. which has been financed in many instances with promissory notesborrowed money which will have to be repaid. Additional notes have been issued to add to the balance sheets of the failed institutions. Finally. In some of the deals. the Bank Board has given the purchasers guarantees against future losses in taking over the institutionssome running 10 years. Thus. the $38.6 billion cost announced on Friday is just a minimum. Moreover. these outlays and the future obligations have a significant Impact on the Federal budget and deficit. Promissory notes. which the Bank Board contends are guaranteed by the Federal Government. add directly and immediately to Federal spending. and to the deficit. The guarantees against future losses also are considered Federal obligations. and become outlays and debt when they are exercised. All in all. we have a situation where the Bank Board is dragging us even deeper into a financial black hole. Yet. under current law it has unprecedented authority to commit the American taxpayer to billions of dollars in obligations. but without any effective control. The Board is even free from the Apportionment Act. which is the Presidents main fiscal management tool. Clearly. we need fundamental changes. not only to solve the immediate problem of some 200 hopelessly insolvent thrifts In which depositors have federally insured funds. but also to prevent any reoccurrence. When the 101st Congress constitutes its committees. we must begin at once to address these problems squarely. The Bush administration must play a constructive role. bringing to us its options. This will take some weeks. perhaps even months. In the meantime. the Bank Board will continue to function without control. In considering how to bring some measure of Immediate protection for the taxpayer. I have written to the Attorney General. requesting him to use his authority as our chief law enforcement officer to determine whether the Bank Board is acting in the public Interest I have specifically recommended that he review the sales announced last week and move for Injunctions against them if they ae not in the public Interest. A copy of my letter Is included. for the information of my colleagues. As we search for a solution to the crisis in the thrift industry. we must remember that the American taxpayer already is paying too much for a bloated and Inefficient Federal Government. We have made some progress in the past several years In curtailing the growth In spending. However. if we are not careful. the S&L crisis will cancel out all our good work of the past several years and put us back on a course of fiscal disaster. U.S. Housz oF REPRESzENATxVES.,the 101st congress convenes today faced with a number of pressing national problems none however carries more potential for damage to our economy than the growing crisis in the thrift industry the events of the past 10 days in which the federal home loan bank board sold dozens of savings and loans at firesale prices only underscores the extent of the problem during 1988 the board took over 222 thrifts with assets totaling 90 billion the cost to the federal governmentthat is the american taxpayeramounts to a minimum of 38 6 billion and probably a good deal more in future years during the previous congress my house banking committee delved into the s l crisis and began the difficult work to find a solution legislation was approved to give the board authority for stopgap measures to prevent a collapse of the federal savings and loan insurance corporation which liabilities for insured accounts in troubled institutions now amount to 60 to 80 billion more than its assets but it is the spectacle of last weeks financial feeding frenzy at the bank board which brings home the nature of the problem and the need for new leadership in this issue contrary to the blandly reassuring statements by the bank board the sales of banke 12 january 3 1989 congre rupt s ls do not end the federal governments liability first in many of the deals the bank board retains part ownership which has been financed in many instances with promissory notesborrowed money which will have to be repaid additional notes have been issued to add to the balance sheets of the failed institutions finally in some of the deals the bank board has given the purchasers guarantees against future losses in taking over the institutionssome running 10 years thus the 38 6 billion cost announced on friday is just a minimum moreover these outlays and the future obligations have a significant impact on the federa