completed his military training at catalina island off the coast of califomia and he served with great distinction l strongly disagree with those who argue that these individuals are not en tied to veterans status let me tell you why all merchant mariners who served between december 7 1941 and december 31 1946 should be granted veterans status once again using mr page as an example while it is true he was on active service after august 15 1945 the japanese did not formally surrender to the united states and its allies until september 2 1945 mr page and the other hundreds of merchant mariners who completed training in 1945 and 1946 at places like kings point st petersburg and catalina island served on ships under the direct military control of the u s navy these merchant marines traveled under sealed orders and their vessels were equipped with an army of military equipment including 50caliber guns in addition the war shipping administration was in control of all ship movements and merchant mariners until december 31 1946 in fact early in the war the director of the selective service gen lewis hershey had ino atructed all draft boards throughout the couno try that service in the merchant marine is so closely allied to service in the armed forces that men found by the local board to be actively engagedi at sea may well be considered as engaged in active defense of the country- such service may propery be considered as tantamount to military service those ships were not taking pleasure cruises they were transporting des perately needed supplies including medicine to countries that had been devastated by war the ships and their crews traveled through heavly mined waters like the mediterranean sea and on more than one occasionr merchant ships were sunk by mines in fact there were at least 11 u s mechant vessels damaged or lost after august 15 1945 one of those was the ss jesse biiilngsley which was hit by a mine off the coast of trieste yugoslavia on nouember 19 1946 one u s merchant mariner lost his life in that explosion not untit december 3t 1946 did president harry truman state during a press conference that he was issuing proclamation 2714 which read in part that although a state of war still exists it is at this time possible to declare and i find it to be in the public interests to declare that hostilities have terminated all other laws that affect world war it veterans use the date december 31 1946 quoting from the veterans administratibn manual chapter ivveterans benefits war period world was ii compries the period from december 7 1941 to december 31 1945 in addition it is important to note that a person could have been inducted into the u s army during the week prior to august 15 1945 and would be rightly considered a world war ii veteran why don t all of our world war ii merchant mariners receive the same consid eration the august 15 1945 date simply has no basis in law in fact the overwhelmlng preponderance of evidence suggests that the correct and only relevant date is december 31 1948 after all that is the date of proclamation 2714 and that is the date used for all other word war ii veterans laws while i dont question the motives of the secretary of the air force for choosing this socalled period of armed conflict justice demands that congress finish the job which secretary aldridge began that is the key purpose of my legislationm to extend veterans status to all merchant mariners who served our countrys war effort between december 7 1941 and december 31 1946 f remarks e 17 while it is true that our merchant seamen received letters ribbons and other citations for their outstanding service they are entitled to more than just these tokens of service they deserve not only our respect but they deserve to be treated in exactly the same manner as all other world war ii veterans the second purpose of this important legis lation is to give the secretary of transportation the authority to provide obsolete vessels to certain nonprofit organizations interested in establishing merchant marine memorials while there are many memorials for those who- sacrificed their lives in the armed forces of this nation there are precious few dedicated to those merchant mariners killed during our nations conflicts as i have already demonstrated it is clear that the outcome of world war ii would have been dramatically different it the u s merchant marine tad not done its job with such courage determination and bravery this provlon will in a small way say to the families of those merchant mariners who lost their ilves that their sacrifice was not in vain and that this nation still honors their gallant efforts under the terms of this provisior to quality for an obsolete vessel an organization would be required to raise at least 100 000 in pd vote funds prior to the enactment of this bill there are nor federd funds authorized and additional fiscal safeguards have been built into the act for instance an obsolete vessel would have to be delivered to the recipient at its current location in its present condition and without cost to the federal government these types of merchant marine memorials are long overdue i would also like to compliment our highly distinguished colleague from marylond congresswoman helen bentley for her tireless dedication to this idea and to the wellbeing of all our merchant seamen now is the time to enact the merchant mariners fairness and memorial act,1980s
1010000377,1989,,"Mr. Speaker. since Its passage. I havent heard 1 of the 72 Members. who voted against the Medicare. Catastrophic Coverage Act express regret over their vote.. but Ive heard complaints from many of those who are sorry they voted for It. Lately. weve been reading more financial reports advising. senior citizens on. how to adjust. their Incomes In 1989 to reduce their tax liabilities. They are coming up with clever strategies to avoid paying the. neow surtax on their Income tax that was imposed on them to pay for a Catastrophic Coverage Act they didnt want. But. this Is one New Years gift from Congress which taxpayingi seniors find. unwelcome. In reducing tax liabilities to avoid the surtax. taxpaying seniors will withdraw moneys availJanuary . 1989 able for benefits. Id like to ask my colleagues if they know that the Catastrophic Coverage Act contains a provision requiring that ""The Secretary would increase the flat premium to replace revenue"" in the case of a revenue shortfall. I fear this might result In not only higher Medicare part B payments. but also higher deductibles. lower levels of service and fewer people covered. The funding mechanism Is selfdefeating. And. there are other perverse effects. At a time when. in many areas of the country. seniors are being asked to reenter the work force. the Medicare Catastrophic Act Is a disincentive to work. If seniors make more money and thus pay more Income taxes. they will be forced to pay this surcharge on their taxes. That reduces the Incentive to workand the incentive to save. as well. The Catastrophic Coverage Act of 1988 might better be called the ""Seniors Antisupply Side Economics Act of 1988."" At a time when society Is craving greater savings and Investment by seniors to cover retirement expenses. the Medicare Catastrophic Act Is a mistake.* Its a raw deal for seniors who have saved and -invested wisely for their later years. Not only will they have to pay income taxes. but also new surcharges on those taxes for a bill that doesnt even encompass longterm care. My constituents are writing and calling my office to protest the surtax and the sharp Increases in Medicare part B. I understand thats happening in other congressional districts. too. The financing mechanisms In the bill are onerous for our taxpaying senior citizens. given the inevitable reduction of taxable Income and assets. they sow the seeds of their own failure. For these reasons. I am introducing a bill to repeal the Medicare CGtastrophIc Act of 1988 and to establish a bipartisan advisory group to study and develop proposals to provide protection against excessive costs of catastrophic Illness beyond the scope of current Medicare coverage. This advisory group would: Study the needs of Medicare beneficiaries for extended acute Illness care beyond what Medicare already provides. Study the differences in acute care experiences of Medicare beneficiaries who have supplemental Insurance or comparable coveragesuch as retirement health benefitscompared to those without such coverage. Study how Medicare benefits may be complimented by Governmentbacked private supplemental Insurance or comparable coverage to protect low income persons not covered by Medicaid. from excessive acute medical care expenses. Study the relative needs of retirees for extended acute care. versus longterm homecare and nursing home Insurance for nonacute care situations. Evaluate short and longterm costs and funding mechanisms of such studies and proposals for providing protection against excessive costs of catastrophic Illness beyond the scope of current Medicare coverage. and Finally. this advisory group would develop proposals to make available extended acute care Insurance protection taking Into consideration and not eliminating existing coverage. supplemental Insurance and comparable coverage. If you too have been faced with seniors complaints. I urge you to cosponsor this legislation. If you have questions or would like to cosponsor this bill. please contact my office.",since its passage i havent heard 1 of the 72 members who voted against the medicare catastrophic coverage act express regret over their vote but ive heard complaints from many of those who are sorry they voted for it lately weve been reading more financial reports advising senior citizens on how to adjust their incomes in 1989 to reduce their tax liabilities they are coming up with clever strategies to avoid paying the neow surtax on their income tax that was imposed on them to pay for a catastrophic coverage act they didnt want but this is one new years gift from congress which taxpayingi seniors find unwelcome in reducing tax liabilities to avoid the surtax taxpaying seniors will withdraw moneys availjanuary 1989 able for benefits id like to ask my colleagues if they know that the catastrophic coverage act contains a provision requiring that the secretary would increase the flat premium to replace revenue in the case of a revenue shortfall i fear this might result in not only higher medicare part b payments but also higher deductibles lower levels of service and fewer people covered the funding mechanism is selfdefeating and there are other perverse effects at a time when in many areas of the country seniors are being asked to reenter the work force the medicare catastrophic act is a disincentive to work if seniors make more money and thus pay more income taxes they will be forced to pay this surcharge on their taxes that reduces the incentive to workand the incentive to save as well the catastrophic coverage act of 1988 might better be called the seniors antisupply side economics act of 1988 at a time when society is craving greater savings and investment by seniors to cover retirement expenses the medicare catastrophic act is a mistake its a raw deal for seniors who have saved and -invested wisely for their later years not only will they have to pay income taxes but also new surcharges on those taxes for a bill that doesnt even encompass longterm care my constituents are writing and calling my office to protest the surtax and the sharp increases in medicare part b i understand thats happening in other congressional districts too the financing mechanisms in the bill are onerous for our taxpaying senior citizens given the inevitable reduction of taxable income and assets they sow the seeds of their own failure for these reasons i am introducing a bill to repeal the medicare cgtastrophic act of 1988 and to establish a bipartisan advisory group to study and develop proposals to provide protection against excessive costs of catastrophic illness beyond the scope of current medicare coverage this advisory group would study the needs of medicare beneficiaries for extended acute illness care beyond what medicare already provides study the differences in acute care experiences of medicare beneficiaries who have supplemental insurance or comparable coveragesuch as retirement health benefitscompared to those without such coverage study how medicare benefits may be complimented by governmentbacked private supplemental insurance or comparable coverage to protect low income persons not covered by medicaid from excessive acute medical care expenses study the relative needs of retirees for extended acute care versus longterm homecare and nursing home insurance for nonacute care situations evaluate short and longterm costs and funding mechanisms of such studies and proposals for providing protection against excessive costs of catastrophic illness beyond the scope of current medicare coverage and finally this advisory group would develop proposals to make available extended acute care insurance protection taking into consideration and not eliminating existing coverage supplemental insurance and comparable coverage if you too have been faced with seniors complaints i urge you to cosponsor this legislation if you have questions or would like to cosponsor this bill please contact my office,1980s
1010000378,1989,,"Mr. Speaker. the 1988 election campaign was very distressing In many respects. When only 60 percent of the eligible voters bother to go to the polls. when millions of dollars In nonreported funds are loosely spread about the political scene. when the cost of running for the House now exceeds what it cost only a few years ago to run for the Senate. and when a sizable portion of the electorate feels cheated out of Its vote. clearly a need for improvement has been demonstrated. and the demand for change and prompt congressional action becomes overwhelming. Today. we are Introducing a major package of reform bills designed to correct many of the abuses that have entered our present election process. Other colleagues will be joining me In introducing additional legislation aimed at the abuses and gaps In present election law. so we will have today a block of bills. sequential. ly numbered. focused on Improving our elsetion process. You. Mr. Speaker. and the chairman of the House Committee on Administration. FRANK ANNUNZIO. have Indicated that this Issue Is a priority for the 101st Congress. and as chairman of the Subcommittee on Elections. I plan to take Immediate action on the proposed legislation. It Is my understanding that the Senate also plans to move ahead rapidly in this area. With determination and good will. we can put In place better laws to govern and direct our elections. which. after all. are the basic building blocks of our democracy. The first bill. Introduced by Chairman AN. NUNZIO. Is the Soft Money Control Act. Although Congress approved public funding for Presidential elections In a deliberate effort to get away from the corrupting Influence of huge Individual. corporate. and other organization contributions. the money raisers In 1988 found various ways to reap In millions of socalled soft dollars to be used in the Presidential campaign. This practice violates the Intent of Congress. and the chairmans Soft Money Control Act will close the loophole through which these funds have found their way Into the Presidential election process. By doing so. this bill will restore the Integrity of the Presdential campaign funding system. a system which has served the country extraordinarily well since its Inception. The second bill. which Chairman ANNUNZIO and I are Introducing along with 15 other origlnal cosponsors. Is the Campaign Cost Reduction and Reform Act. This measure Is Identical to legislation I Introduced In the last Congress and offers an Innovative and comprehensive approach to reducing the cost of campaigns. thus reducing the need to raise huge amounts of money. The cost of congressional election campaigns Is out of control. Not only are chal. lengers placed In an almost Impossible financlal situation. but Incumbents are forced to spend an enormous amount of time raising money Instead of doing their jobs. The possibility for corruption and undue Influence accelerates with the rising cost of campaigning. This legislation offers specific Incentives to candidates to limit their expenditures in return for substantial reductions In campaign costs. Again. I believe that this Is the most pragmatic and costefficient approach to a serious problem which no longer can simply be debated. I know this Is a very emotional and controversial Issue. My subcommittee has held numerous public hearings on the subject and I have been working for several years to develop a consensus within the Congress. Everyone recognizes the problem and almost everyone has his own solution. The Campaign Cost Reduction and Reform Act offers a compromise as well as a major step forward In reforming the process. Other approaches will be Introduced and will receive full consideration from my subcommittee. I have also Introduced today. along with Chairman ANNUNZIO. the National Voter RegIstration Act. A postelection New York Times/ CBS News poll indicated that nearly 40 percent of those who did not vote would have if they had been registered. Improving the registration process Is the most Important legislative action we can take to Increase voter turnout. The bill provides that an eligible citizen will automatically be registered to vote when he or she applies for or renews a drivers license. unless the Individual specifically declines. National statistics indicate that 87 percent of the eligible voters in the United States hold drivers ligenses and perhaps another 2 or 3 percent hold Identification cards which have been issued at the drivers license office. Thus. the National Voter Registration Act. when fully implemented by. the States. will reach 90 percent of the eligible public. To reach the remaining 10 percent. my bill calls for mall regltration procedures and agency registration. The legislation also adjusts the purging process to make It less burdensome for the occasional voter. This measure Improves the registration process while extremely limiting the opportunity for fraud. It Is the product of extensive hearings and consultations with election officials and voting rights activists conducted by my subcommittee last year. I believe it Is a pragmatic. costefficient and politically sound approach to a serious problem for our democracy. Other bills. IncludIng legislation providing for election day registration. will be Introduced and will receive full consideration. I am reintroducing today my uniform poll closing bill. legislation which has already twice passed the House. Chairman ANNUNZIO. together with the ranking member on my subcommittee. BIL THOMAS. have joined me In cosponsoring this measure. The uniform poll closing bill. along with the network agreement not to project an election winner until the polls have closed in a specific State. will ensure that all the eligible voters In the continental United States have a fair and equitable opportunity to make their choices before the networks proclaim a winner. While the networks. following their policy. were still able to project the election at 9:20 p.m. eastern standard time because the polls in States with a sufficient number of electoral votes had closed by that hour. millions of voters were left frustratE is January . 1989 ed and feeling disenfranchised since their pols had. not yet closed. Enactment of the uniform poll closing bill. in conjunction with the network agreement. would put an end to this early projection problem since all the polls in the continental United States would close at exactly the same hour. thus negating the opportunity for an early projection. Additional bills which are being Introduced as part of this reform package will address such issues as money bundling and tax credits for political contributions. I anticipate other measures on this same general subject will be offered by Members on both sides of the aisle. and I assure my colleagues that the Subcommittee on Elections will consider all such proposals with great care. Because this is an Inherently controversial area. it will be extremely difficult to pass meaningful reform without fair and equitable compromises. I will be circulating more detailed information on these bills over the next several weeks. and I welcome cosponsors along with any suggestions or recommendations. You. Mr. Speaker. and Chairman ANNUNZIO have given me the ""full steam ahead"" signal on this legislation. and. as soon as the committees are officially organized. my Subcommittee on Elections will get cracking.",the 1988 election campaign was very distressing in many respects when only 60 percent of the eligible voters bother to go to the polls when millions of dollars in nonreported funds are loosely spread about the political scene when the cost of running for the house now exceeds what it cost only a few years ago to run for the senate and when a sizable portion of the electorate feels cheated out of its vote clearly a need for improvement has been demonstrated and the demand for change and prompt congressional action becomes overwhelming today we are introducing a major package of reform bills designed to correct many of the abuses that have entered our present election process other colleagues will be joining me in introducing additional legislation aimed at the abuses and gaps in present election law so we will have today a block of bills sequential ly numbered focused on improving our elsetion process you and the chairman of the house committee on administration frank annunzio have indicated that this issue is a priority for the 101st congress and as chairman of the subcommittee on elections i plan to take immediate action on the proposed legislation it is my understanding that the senate also plans to move ahead rapidly in this area with determination and good will we can put in place better laws to govern and direct our elections which after all are the basic building blocks of our democracy the first bill introduced by chairman an nunzio is the soft money control act although congress approved public funding for presidential elections in a deliberate effort to get away from the corrupting influence of huge individual corporate and other organization contributions the money raisers in 1988 found various ways to reap in millions of socalled soft dollars to be used in the presidential campaign this practice violates the intent of congress and the chairmans soft money control act will close the loophole through which these funds have found their way into the presidential election process by doing so this bill will restore the integrity of the presdential campaign funding system a system which has served the country extraordinarily well since its inception the second bill which chairman annunzio and i are introducing along with 15 other origlnal cosponsors is the campaign cost reduction and reform act this measure is identical to legislation i introduced in the last congress and offers an innovative and comprehensive approach to reducing the cost of campaigns thus reducing the need to raise huge amounts of money the cost of congressional election campaigns is out of control not only are chal lengers placed in an almost impossible financlal situation but incumbents are forced to spend an enormous amount of time raising money instead of doing their jobs the possibility for corruption and undue influence accelerates with the rising cost of campaigning this legislation offers specific incentives to candidates to limit their expenditures in return for substantial reductions in campaign costs again i believe that this is the most pragmatic and costefficient approach to a serious problem which no longer can simply be debated i know this is a very emotional and controversial issue my subcommittee has held numerous public hearings on the subject and i have been working for several years to develop a consensus within the congress everyone recognizes the problem and almost everyone has his own solution the campaign cost reduction and reform act offers a compromise as well as a major step forward in reforming the process other approaches will be introduced and will receive full consideration from my subcommittee i have also introduced today along with chairman annunzio the national voter registration act a postelection new york times cbs news poll indicated that nearly 40 percent of those who did not vote would have if they had been registered improving the registration process is the most important legislative action we can take to increase voter turnout the bill provides that an eligible citizen will automatically be registered to vote when he or she applies for or renews a drivers license unless the individual specifically declines national statistics indicate that 87 percent of the eligible voters in the united states hold drivers ligenses and perhaps another 2 or 3 percent hold identification cards which have been issued at the drivers license office thus the national voter registration act when fully implemented by the states will reach 90 percent of the eligible public to reach the remaining 10 percent my bill calls for mall regltration procedures and agency registration the legislation also adjusts the purging process to make it less burdensome for the occasional voter this measure improves the registration process while extremely limiting the opportunity for fraud it is the product of extensive hearings and consultations with election officials and voting rights activists conducted by my subcommittee last year i believe it is a pragmatic costefficient and politically sound approach to a serious problem for our democracy other bills including legislation providing for election day registration will be introduced and will receive full consideration i am reintroducing today my uniform poll closing bill legislation which has already twice passed the house chairman annunzio together with the ranking member on my subcommittee bil thomas have joined me in cosponsoring this measure the uniform poll closing bill along with the network agreement not to project an election winner until the polls have closed in a specific state will ensure that all the eligible voters in the continental united states have a fair and equitable opportunity to make their choices before the networks proclaim a winner while the networks following their policy were still able to project the election at 9 20 p m eastern standard time because the polls in states with a sufficient number of electoral votes had closed by that hour millions of voters were left frustrate is january 1989 ed and feeling disenfranchised since their pols had not yet closed enactment of the uniform poll closing bill in conjunction with the network agreement would put an end to this early projection problem since all the polls in the continental united states would close at exactly the same hour thus negating the opportunity for an early projection additional bills which are being introduced as part of this reform package will address such issues as money bundling and tax credits for political contributions i anticipate other measures on this same general subject will be offered by members on both sides of the aisle and i assure my colleagues that the subcommittee on elections will consider all such proposals with great care because this is an inherently controversial area it will be extremely difficult to pass meaningful reform without fair and equitable compromises i will be circulating more detailed information on these bills over the next several weeks and i welcome cosponsors along with any suggestions or recommendations you and chairman annunzio have given me the full steam ahead signal on this legislation and as soon as the committees are officially organized my subcommittee on elections will get cracking,1980s
1010000379,1989,,"Mr. Speaker. I am introducing legislation today to exempt certain taxpayers from the Internal Revenue Service User Fee Program enacted as part of the $987 budget reconciliation legislation. My legislation. restores some fairness to retired senior citizens who have been penalized bythe new User Fee Program. Mr. Speaker. the 1987 reconciliation legisla. tion authorized the Internal Revenue Service to establish a User Fee Program. The bill envisioned that the IRS would set up a series o fees for certain services which the agency had previously been providing for froe. For example. if a taxpayer now requests a private letter ruling. the IRS will generally charge that taxpayer at least $200. The legislation. did give the IRS some discretionary authority to reduce or waive the fees in certain Instances. however. One year before enactment of the 1987 legIslation. In the 1986 Tax Reform Act. Congress agreed to repeal the socalled 3year recovery rule. under which taxpayers who contributed to their own pension. were entitled to. recover their own contributions tax free so long as they recovered their total contributions within 3 years. After the employees contribu. tions were recovered. the pension was fully taxable. The 1986 act replaced this simplified scheme with. a far more complicated method ot annuity taxation. In general. a taxpayer who contributes to his own pension. must calculate his ""exclusion: ration"" to determine the portion. ot his pensiors that Is subject to tax. The formula used for calculating the exclusion ratio Is extremely complicated and requires taxpayers to use annuity tables published by the litemal Revenue Service. It has recently come to my atten.on that the IRS has used. the generic authority. contained in the 1987 legislation to impose user fees to charge taxpayers who need to calculate their exclusion ratio. Mr. Speaker. this Is adding Insult to Injury. I opposed the repeal of the 3year recovery rule. I hope that Congress will someday overturn that mistaken decision. But to add to the unfairness of repealing the 3year recovery rule a fee to calculate a tax liability legally due the Government is an outrage. To its credit. the IRS has reduced the fees it could have charged. and is only charging $50. Still. there is an element of unfairness In the IRS asking taxpayers to use an extremely complicated formula and risk making a costly error or else charging retired employees on a fixed pension $50 to make this calculation for them. Therefore. I have Introduced legislation prohibiting the IRS from charging any fee for calculating a taxpayers exclusion ratio. My bill also clarifies that any fees paid by taxpayers during the last tax season will be deductible. The provisions apply to requests made after Mr. Speaker. this legislation is only fair. Many of us have heard from our constituents with complaints over the complexity of Income tax forms. and the $50 fe3 is merely more complexity and more unfairness in their minds. This legislation deserves strong support. and I will be urging my colleagues on the Ways and Means Committee to move expeditiously on it in the coming months.",i am introducing legislation today to exempt certain taxpayers from the internal revenue service user fee program enacted as part of the 987 budget reconciliation legislation my legislation restores some fairness to retired senior citizens who have been penalized bythe new user fee program the 1987 reconciliation legisla tion authorized the internal revenue service to establish a user fee program the bill envisioned that the irs would set up a series o fees for certain services which the agency had previously been providing for froe for example if a taxpayer now requests a private letter ruling the irs will generally charge that taxpayer at least 200 the legislation did give the irs some discretionary authority to reduce or waive the fees in certain instances however one year before enactment of the 1987 legislation in the 1986 tax reform act congress agreed to repeal the socalled 3year recovery rule under which taxpayers who contributed to their own pension were entitled to recover their own contributions tax free so long as they recovered their total contributions within 3 years after the employees contribu tions were recovered the pension was fully taxable the 1986 act replaced this simplified scheme with a far more complicated method ot annuity taxation in general a taxpayer who contributes to his own pension must calculate his exclusion ration to determine the portion ot his pensiors that is subject to tax the formula used for calculating the exclusion ratio is extremely complicated and requires taxpayers to use annuity tables published by the litemal revenue service it has recently come to my atten on that the irs has used the generic authority contained in the 1987 legislation to impose user fees to charge taxpayers who need to calculate their exclusion ratio this is adding insult to injury i opposed the repeal of the 3year recovery rule i hope that congress will someday overturn that mistaken decision but to add to the unfairness of repealing the 3year recovery rule a fee to calculate a tax liability legally due the government is an outrage to its credit the irs has reduced the fees it could have charged and is only charging 50 still there is an element of unfairness in the irs asking taxpayers to use an extremely complicated formula and risk making a costly error or else charging retired employees on a fixed pension 50 to make this calculation for them therefore i have introduced legislation prohibiting the irs from charging any fee for calculating a taxpayers exclusion ratio my bill also clarifies that any fees paid by taxpayers during the last tax season will be deductible the provisions apply to requests made after this legislation is only fair many of us have heard from our constituents with complaints over the complexity of income tax forms and the 50 fe3 is merely more complexity and more unfairness in their minds this legislation deserves strong support and i will be urging my colleagues on the ways and means committee to move expeditiously on it in the coming months,1980s
1010000380,1989,,"Mr. Speaker. our former colleague. Jack Kemp. who. has been nominated to be Secretary of the Department of Housing and Urban Development. worked diligently during his years In Congress to pass legislation to establish enterprise zones. As Secretary. I know he will want to pursue this most worthy goal. To ensure that the development of enterprise zones remains a priority for Members of Congress In the 101st. I am reintroducing this proposal today f think. the time has come to pass this needed legislation and look forward to working"" with my colleagues and the Secretarydesignate. Jack Kemp. on this most Important issue.",our former colleague jack kemp who has been nominated to be secretary of the department of housing and urban development worked diligently during his years in congress to pass legislation to establish enterprise zones as secretary i know he will want to pursue this most worthy goal to ensure that the development of enterprise zones remains a priority for members of congress in the 101st i am reintroducing this proposal today f think the time has come to pass this needed legislation and look forward to working with my colleagues and the secretarydesignate jack kemp on this most important issue,1980s
1010000381,1989,,"Mr. Speaker. today. on- the first day of the One Hundred First Congress. I am Introducing the Peaceful Atom Act. a bill to. bar conversio of cMilan nuclear reactors to military use. Joining me are my colleagues from Oregon. Mr. DEFzie and Mr. AuCOIN. Since the beginning of our development of peaceful atomic energy. we have kept a wall between military and civilian uses of this power. That wall lets the whole world join us In enjoying the benefits of peaceful power. while discouraging the spread of nuclear weapons. Im sorry to say that wall is In danger. President Reagan has ordered the Nuclear Regulatory Commission to draw up plans for Federal takeover of civilian plants in a defense emergency. And our Department of Energy [DOE] wants to develop the technology to turn a reactor designed for civilian use Into a producer of bomb fuels. It has Its eyes on a halfbuilt Washington Public Power Supply System (WPPSS] plant In. the Pacific Northwest The Idea of converting the WPPSS reactor sends a special chill down the backs: of Oregonians. From the Northwest electric ratepayers point of view. converting WPPSS makes about as much sense as shaking a homets nest. It Is sure to trigger new law suits from WPPSS bondholders. At worst. it could send the Bonneville Power Administration scrambling to pay off the multibillion dollar WPPSS1 debt with nowhere to turn but the taxpayers. The effect on the regions economy Is almost too awful to think about. And there are more environmental wrinkles involved than you can count. There Is pollution from tritium reprocessing pollution that has contaminated DOEs Savannah River Plant with toxic mercury and giver it rain that isnt safe to. drink. There are questions about the plants impact on fish and wildlife along the Columbia River and all along the Northwest power grid. There are concerns. valid ones. in every town along Interstate 84 In eastern Oregon. about shipping radioactive fuel. wastes. and bomb materials through those canyons and mountains. There are unanswered questions about catastrophic accidents. since the enriched fuel at a defense facility offers dangers civilian plants dont have to be designed for. dangers like a ""recriticality"" it the fuel melts. There are emerging safety concerns. like the plants ability to withstand earthquakes we didnt think were possible when the plant was designed. And I have to wonder why. if WPPSS conversion is such a good idea. DOE is afraid to turn the plans over to the Nuclear Regulatory Commission for their environmental and safety approval in a licensing proceeding. But the biggest issue of all. for all Americans. Is nuclear nonproliferation. Plans for WPPSS conversion and reactor takeovers are sending a terrible message to the many coun. ties that have nuclear plants but do not have the bomb: nuclear weapons are just within your grasp. We cant expect other countries to resist the temptation to raid their domestic nuclear industry if we give In ourselves. In 1983. Congress amended the Atomic Energy Act to prohibit scavenging bomb material from American civilian plant wastes. Now we need to reaffirm that action by barring military use of any plant designed for civilian ends. Through. treaties and diplomacy. we have taken great strides in the past few years to lessen the threat of nuclear holocaust from the weapons of the superpowers. But we must not ignore the parallel threat from proliferation of nuclear weapons among other nations. The E19 Peaceful Atom Act. by Itself. will not end the threat. But It will tell the world the United States Is serious about keeping the nuclear peace. I urge my colleagues to support this bill.",today on- the first day of the one hundred first congress i am introducing the peaceful atom act a bill to bar conversio of cmilan nuclear reactors to military use joining me are my colleagues from oregon mr defzie and mr aucoin since the beginning of our development of peaceful atomic energy we have kept a wall between military and civilian uses of this power that wall lets the whole world join us in enjoying the benefits of peaceful power while discouraging the spread of nuclear weapons im sorry to say that wall is in danger president reagan has ordered the nuclear regulatory commission to draw up plans for federal takeover of civilian plants in a defense emergency and our department of energy doe wants to develop the technology to turn a reactor designed for civilian use into a producer of bomb fuels it has its eyes on a halfbuilt washington public power supply system wppss plant in the pacific northwest the idea of converting the wppss reactor sends a special chill down the backs of oregonians from the northwest electric ratepayers point of view converting wppss makes about as much sense as shaking a homets nest it is sure to trigger new law suits from wppss bondholders at worst it could send the bonneville power administration scrambling to pay off the multibillion dollar wppss1 debt with nowhere to turn but the taxpayers the effect on the regions economy is almost too awful to think about and there are more environmental wrinkles involved than you can count there is pollution from tritium reprocessing pollution that has contaminated does savannah river plant with toxic mercury and giver it rain that isnt safe to drink there are questions about the plants impact on fish and wildlife along the columbia river and all along the northwest power grid there are concerns valid ones in every town along interstate 84 in eastern oregon about shipping radioactive fuel wastes and bomb materials through those canyons and mountains there are unanswered questions about catastrophic accidents since the enriched fuel at a defense facility offers dangers civilian plants dont have to be designed for dangers like a recriticality it the fuel melts there are emerging safety concerns like the plants ability to withstand earthquakes we didnt think were possible when the plant was designed and i have to wonder why if wppss conversion is such a good idea doe is afraid to turn the plans over to the nuclear regulatory commission for their environmental and safety approval in a licensing proceeding but the biggest issue of all for all americans is nuclear nonproliferation plans for wppss conversion and reactor takeovers are sending a terrible message to the many coun ties that have nuclear plants but do not have the bomb nuclear weapons are just within your grasp we cant expect other countries to resist the temptation to raid their domestic nuclear industry if we give in ourselves in 1983 congress amended the atomic energy act to prohibit scavenging bomb material from american civilian plant wastes now we need to reaffirm that action by barring military use of any plant designed for civilian ends through treaties and diplomacy we have taken great strides in the past few years to lessen the threat of nuclear holocaust from the weapons of the superpowers but we must not ignore the parallel threat from proliferation of nuclear weapons among other nations the e19 peaceful atom act by itself will not end the threat but it will tell the world the united states is serious about keeping the nuclear peace i urge my colleagues to support this bill,1980s
1010000382,1989,,"Mr. Speaker. today I introduced the Allies Fair Share Defense Act of 1989. a measure to ensure that our NATO allies and Japan contribute a fair share to provide for their own security and defense. In 1951. Dwight Eisenhower viewed American aid for NATO as essential. but he stated. ""If in 10 years. all American troops stationed In Europe for national defense purposes have not been returned to the United States. then this whole project will have failed."" Now. more than four decades after the end of World War II. the number of U.S. troops stationed in Europe Is 340.243321.896 on land and 18.347 afloatnearly 89.000 more than a decade ago. Our allies forces. in contrast. have barely increased. The United States troop presence in Japan Is 48.940with an additional 22.319 afloat In the East Asian Pacific. All told. 370.836411.502 including those afloatmembers of the U.S. military were stationed outside the United States as of A major U.S. role In the defense of our allies was warranted when. as they emerged from World War I. they were weak economically and fragile politically. The economic and political strength and maturity of our allies now requires that they should take more financial responsibility for their own defense and the worldwide defense of democracy. We are subsidizing their defense while they are subsidizing their exports. And Its costing us millions of jobs. I Under the protective shield of the United States. European countries. and Japan have been able to modernize their industrial plants. while the United States now has an estimated ttade deficit of $152 billion. I believe the time has come to send a clear signal to our allies that we are serious about achieving a better balance of defense expenditures. Secretary of Defense Carlucci has said that our NATO allies ""could and should do more."" Rozanne Ridgway. Assistant Secretary of State for European and Canadian Affairs. and George Bader. Principal Deputy for European and NATO policy in the Pentagons Office of International Security Policy. each insisted during testimony to the defense burdensharing panel that increasing allied contributions to military defense remains high on the diplomatIc agenda. The Senate Appropriations Committees fiscal year 1988 military construcilon bill repeatedly emphasized that the deep cuts In projects In Europe and Japan reflected a fundamental demand that the allies pay more and the Pentagon pay less of the total cost of alliance defense. Recognizing that Italy spends about 2 percent of its gross domestic product [GDP] on defense. Italian Defense Minister Valedo Zanone wants to raise Italian defense spendIng by $25 billion over the next decade to modernize the Italian Armed Forces. NATOs Commander of Allied Forces Baltic Approaches. Lt. Gen. Paul Thorsen. resigned from his post and from the Danish Army last year. attacking the ""skinflint"" Danish politicians and administrators for failing to support NATO and for ""continually undermining Danish defense."" A farreaching report scheduled to be released by the North Atlantic Assembly. a group of influential political leaders from major NATO nations. states. in essence. that Europe must do more for its own defense. While I am not suggesting that any one of these leaders might support my legislation. It is clear that these concerns deserve attention and response. Accountability must be added to our allies promises. Our allies must share fairly and fully the risks. burdens. and responsibilities. as well as the benefits of our common efforts. The United States devotes 6.7 percent of Its gross domestic product [GOP] to defense. whereas the member nations of NATO. on the average. spend approximately 3.3 percent of the GDP on defense. and Japanese allocate only slightly more than 1 percent. In fiscal year 1985. a comparison of defense spending and population showed that the United States spent $1.079 per person on defense. NATO allies spent $255 per person. and Japan spent $113 per person. In 1977. each of the NATO allies pledged to annually Increase defense expenditures by 3 percent after inflation. On average. the allies have failed to meet the 3percent spending objective In each year for the period 1978 to 1985the total average Increase for those years being 2.2 percent. For these same years. U.S. defense spending rose by 5.7 percent per year. Japan during this period raised its defense spending 5.4 percent. but still committed only 1 percent of Its GDP to defense. While I recognize that some NATO allies. such as Greece and Turkey. annually exceed the 3percent objective and that NATO nations and Japan each donate their land and natural resources and permit foreign troops to live in their countries. and the NATO allies contribute the majority of aircraft. tanks. ships. and troops to the alliance. I would argue that In the postINF world. our present conventional defense capabilities are dangerously Inadequate. Mr. Speaker. my bill Is not designed to terminate Americas role in assisting with the defense of NATO nations. Japan. South Korea. or any of our democratic allies worldwide. We will continue to maintain a strong political. economic. and social attachment to our allies. What we want Is for our allies to fulfill their repeated promises to the United States with regard to Improving their ownand our commondefense capabilities. My bill Is designed to encourage NATO nations and Japan to Improve their conventional defense capabilities. because the continued reliance on the early use of nuclear weapons is diametrically opposed to the national security interests of this country and the security interests of our allies. The measure establishes a permanent ceiling on United States forces assigned to permanent duty In NATO countries and Japan and provides for the resolution of United States forces In these countries under certain circumstances. The ""Allies Fair Share Defense Act of 1989"" sets a permanent ceiling of 326.414 personnel of the Armed Forces of the United States assigned to permanent duty ashore In member nations of NATO and 65.000 personnel assigned to Japan. Beginning in 1992. and continuing for 3 years. the troop ceiling would be reduced by 30.000 per year for our NATO allies and 7.000 per year for Japan. unless. during the previous fiscal year. each of these countries has Increased Its defense spending by Its real economic growth plus 1 percent. For example. If West Germany were to realIze real economic growth of 1.5 percent In 1990. the following year the government in Bonn would be required to budget an Increase of at least 2.5 percent for West German defense spending In 1991. In contrast to the 1977 NATO pledge to increase annual defense expenditures by 3 percent above inflation. which the NATO nations have consistently failed to do. this approach would take Into account the direct relationship between economic performance and te relative ability of each ally to increase its cotribution to the alliance. In recognition of the fact that military capabilities are also an Important indicator of the allies ability to share the NATO burden. the member nations of NATO may also prevent the troop reductions by meeting three output goals the allies have set for themselves. These goals are not ones I seek to impose on the alliance. but are objectives they have repeatedly pledged to meet in order to increase the sustanability of the conventional deterrent. My bill simply tells our allies to ""put your money where you mouth Is and meet your commitments."" The first alternative goal for our NATO allies is to reduce by 20 percent the gap between conventional munitions on hand as of January 1. 1989. and the NATO goal of obtaining and maintaining a 30 days munitions supply level. An Increase of 20 percent Is established for each year that this amendment would be In effect. aiming at 100 percent compliance over 5 years. However. the measure applies only to a 3year period In order to give Congress and our allies a chance to review Its effectiveness and the progress being made to comply with its provisions.",today i introduced the allies fair share defense act of 1989 a measure to ensure that our nato allies and japan contribute a fair share to provide for their own security and defense in 1951 dwight eisenhower viewed american aid for nato as essential but he stated if in 10 years all american troops stationed in europe for national defense purposes have not been returned to the united states then this whole project will have failed now more than four decades after the end of world war ii the number of u s troops stationed in europe is 340 243321 896 on land and 18 347 afloatnearly 89 000 more than a decade ago our allies forces in contrast have barely increased the united states troop presence in japan is 48 940with an additional 22 319 afloat in the east asian pacific all told 370 836411 502 including those afloatmembers of the u s military were stationed outside the united states as of a major u s role in the defense of our allies was warranted when as they emerged from world war i they were weak economically and fragile politically the economic and political strength and maturity of our allies now requires that they should take more financial responsibility for their own defense and the worldwide defense of democracy we are subsidizing their defense while they are subsidizing their exports and its costing us millions of jobs i under the protective shield of the united states european countries and japan have been able to modernize their industrial plants while the united states now has an estimated ttade deficit of 152 billion i believe the time has come to send a clear signal to our allies that we are serious about achieving a better balance of defense expenditures secretary of defense carlucci has said that our nato allies could and should do more rozanne ridgway assistant secretary of state for european and canadian affairs and george bader principal deput