1010000403,1989,,Mr. Speaker. today I am introducing the Fair Labor Standards Amendments of 1989. to bring economic equity to lowwage workers. It has been 8 years since the Federal minimum wage was raised to the current level of $3.35 an hour. Since that time the cost of living has risen more than 30 per. cent. Thus In todays dollars. the current minimum wage Is worth only $2.56. My bill will begin to restore the purchasing power of the Federal minimum wage by providing for 3 Incremental wage rate Increases to $4.65 by Raising the minimum wage Is also a matter of basic human dignity. If a person Is hungry. he or she should be able to provide themselves with a decent meal. If a person Is homeless. he or she should be able to provide themselves and their families with decent and adequate shelter. Increasing the minimum wage Is a matter of fundamental economic justice. This bill seeks to continue the national policy established more than 50 years ago of Insuring a fair days pay for a fair days work. Its purpose is to restore a floor under wages. Its objective Is the maintenance of the minimum standard of living necessary for health. efficiency. and general wellbeing of workers. My proposal not only provide for Incremental Increases in the minimum wage. it also Incorporates provisions adopted last year by the Committee on Education and Labor. Spociti. cally. the bill provides for an Increase In the socalled small business exemption from the current $382.500 to $500.000 of annual sales or business. In addition. the current 40 percent tip credit provision in the FLSA Is raised to 45 percent a year after enactment. and to 50 percent the following year and thereafter. These provisions represent a substantial compromise In addressing the concerns of small businesses. Suffice it to say. there Is no further necessity to modify or revise the bill which I am Introducing. A final provision creates a 5 member Minimum Wage Review Board to recommend future adjustments to the Congress. Every 2 years the Bodrd would review current economic data and submit to the Congress a recommendation for a minimum wage adjustment to become effective In the following year. with an estimate of the economic Impact of Implementing such an adjustment. The provision Is not selfexecuting. nor is the Congress required to take any action. The recommendstion Is merely advisory. The provision was Included to generate a serious dialog on how to keep the issue of the minimum wage before thd Congress in a systematic manner. It provides for periodic. objective scrutiny of economic conditions on which to base reasoned judgments as to whether or not adjustments should be made in the minimum wage. It requires this Information to be transmitted to the Congress on a regular basis. but does not mandate a change In the wage rate. I think the concept has significant medt. I hope it will receive serious consideration. A brief summary of the bill follows:,today i am introducing the fair labor standards amendments of 1989 to bring economic equity to lowwage workers it has been 8 years since the federal minimum wage was raised to the current level of 3 35 an hour since that time the cost of living has risen more than 30 per cent thus in todays dollars the current minimum wage is worth only 2 56 my bill will begin to restore the purchasing power of the federal minimum wage by providing for 3 incremental wage rate increases to 4 65 by raising the minimum wage is also a matter of basic human dignity if a person is hungry he or she should be able to provide themselves with a decent meal if a person is homeless he or she should be able to provide themselves and their families with decent and adequate shelter increasing the minimum wage is a matter of fundamental economic justice this bill seeks to continue the national policy established more than 50 years ago of insuring a fair days pay for a fair days work its purpose is to restore a floor under wages its objective is the maintenance of the minimum standard of living necessary for health efficiency and general wellbeing of workers my proposal not only provide for incremental increases in the minimum wage it also incorporates provisions adopted last year by the committee on education and labor spociti cally the bill provides for an increase in the socalled small business exemption from the current 382 500 to 500 000 of annual sales or business in addition the current 40 percent tip credit provision in the flsa is raised to 45 percent a year after enactment and to 50 percent the following year and thereafter these provisions represent a substantial compromise in addressing the concerns of small businesses suffice it to say there is no further necessity to modify or revise the bill which i am introducing a final provision creates a 5 member minimum wage review board to recommend future adjustments to the congress every 2 years the bodrd would review current economic data and submit to the congress a recommendation for a minimum wage adjustment to become effective in the following year with an estimate of the economic impact of implementing such an adjustment the provision is not selfexecuting nor is the congress required to take any action the recommendstion is merely advisory the provision was included to generate a serious dialog on how to keep the issue of the minimum wage before thd congress in a systematic manner it provides for periodic objective scrutiny of economic conditions on which to base reasoned judgments as to whether or not adjustments should be made in the minimum wage it requires this information to be transmitted to the congress on a regular basis but does not mandate a change in the wage rate i think the concept has significant medt i hope it will receive serious consideration a brief summary of the bill follows,1980s
1010000404,1989,,Mr. Speaker. I rise today to inform the House that I am reintroducing several bills that will make life better for millions of Medicare patients. In particular. I am introducing four bills that concentrate on the detection and treatment of breast cancer. provide longterm care services for all senior citizens. and to provide Medicare coverage for seniors who need hearing. dental. and vision services. Mr. Speaker. breast cancer is an insidious disease that Is predicted to take the life of at least 42.000 American women In 1989. In addition. the National Cancer Institute estimates that breast cancer will strike at least 1 woman out of every 10 some time In their lives. However. perhaps the most frustrating aspect of this problem Is that while early detection Is technologically feasible. it Is not affordable for most American women. The Office of TechE 40 January 3. 1989 CONGR nology Assessment has estimated that the cost of a standard mammogram to be approximately $100. Most cider women19 percent of whom receive an average of only $421 of Social Security benefits per monthmust make a decision to spend as much as onefourth of their monthly Income on a needed medical procedure. or to pay the rent. As a result. I have reintroduced H.R. 2935. a bill of mine from the 100th Congress. that will provide Medicare coverage for annual mammographic screenings for all women coverage by Medicare. White I recognize that passage of the Medicare catastrophic prevention bill last year provide for coverage of mammograms. it Is stil considered medically insufficient to cover the true needs of older women. My bill proposes to provide annual coveragethe medically recommended coverageinstead of the biannual coverage passed in the Medicare catastrophic bill. In addition. I have reintroduced H.R. 671 from the 100th Congress. This bill will ensure proper treatment of breast cancer by all doctors who receive Medicare payments for the services they provide to older Americans. Specifically. this bill calls for the proper amount and quality of treatment information to be made available for all breast cancer patients. American women need to know that the old days of radical treatments as the cure for every case of breast cancer are gone and that alternative treatments are available which do not disfigure or cost as much for the Amerloan taxpayer. Passage of this bill will mean nothing more difficult for doctors than providing a booklet from a respected breast cancer organization that explains the types of options and their applicability as well as their advantages and disadvantages. This Is a humane and sensible idea whos time has come. Mr. Speaker. I am also reintroducing H.I679 from the last Congress. This bill Is a true comprehensive longterm care bill that covers all the needs of our ser2or citizens. My bill will cover nursing home costs. congregate home services. communitybased mental health care. homemaking or homehelp services. and adult day care or day hospital services. As we all witnessed last Congress. longterm care is an Issue that requires immediate attenlion by the Congress. 1he American people want longterm care services from the Government and they have stated overwhelmingly that they are willing to pay for them. I strongly urge the Congress to consider this important bill. The fourth bill I am reintroducing is H.R. 678. a bill to provide Medicare coverage for certain vision. dental. and hearing services. This bill will provide coverage for vision. hearing. and dental examinations as well as all needs regarding the acquisition of eyeglasses. hearing aids. dentures. and other dental needs. As all older Americans know. the cost of these services can be an enormous expense that requires them to make painful choices regarding their limited Incomes. My legislation will enable all older Americans to live more enjoyable lives. Mr. Speaker. I believe all of us in this Chamber have the desire to improve lil for older Americans. I am hopeful that the 101st Congress will prove to be a watershed for aging Issues In general. but for Improvements in the Medicare system In particular. The passage of my four bills will help all older Americans and I ask that the Members of the historESSIONAL RECORD - Extensioms of Remarks ic 101st Congress consider these bills as soon as possible.,i rise today to inform the house that i am reintroducing several bills that will make life better for millions of medicare patients in particular i am introducing four bills that concentrate on the detection and treatment of breast cancer provide longterm care services for all senior citizens and to provide medicare coverage for seniors who need hearing dental and vision services breast cancer is an insidious disease that is predicted to take the life of at least 42 000 american women in 1989 in addition the national cancer institute estimates that breast cancer will strike at least 1 woman out of every 10 some time in their lives however perhaps the most frustrating aspect of this problem is that while early detection is technologically feasible it is not affordable for most american women the office of teche 40 january 3 1989 congr nology assessment has estimated that the cost of a standard mammogram to be approximately 100 most cider women19 percent of whom receive an average of only 421 of social security benefits per monthmust make a decision to spend as much as onefourth of their monthly income on a needed medical procedure or to pay the rent as a result i have reintroduced h r 2935 a bill of mine from the 100th congress that will provide medicare coverage for annual mammographic screenings for all women coverage by medicare white i recognize that passage of the medicare catastrophic prevention bill last year provide for coverage of mammograms it is stil considered medically insufficient to cover the true needs of older women my bill proposes to provide annual coveragethe medically recommended coverageinstead of the biann